ngular number being used among the Greeks as well as the Latins for the dual or plural."}, {"id": "pous-note1", "translation": "in the manuscripts sometimes πους [foot] is written contrary to the rule of Herodian, On monosyllables p. 14 extr. and of Apollonius, On adverbs p. 544, 31 and other grammarians, about which see Thes. vol. 6 p. 1540."}, {"id": "pous-note2", "translation": "With light foot the swift-moving seat"}, {"id": "pous-note3", "translation": "Pursuing the guiding axle-boxes"}, {"id": "pous-note4", "translation": "where he restored ποδοῖν [the two feet]."}, {"id": "pous-note5", "translation": "Who with swift foot of leaping"}]}